# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Line endings.
#
# This project is developed on Windows and deployed to Linux. Without this file
# git would store the shell scripts and Dockerfile with CRLF endings, and the
# VPS would then fail with either:
#     /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
#     $'\r': command not found
# which is the classic "works on my machine" deploy break.
#
# `eol=lf` normalises every text file to LF in the repository AND in the working
# tree, so what is committed is exactly what runs on the server.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts, containers and config — must never be CRLF.
*.sh          text eol=lf
*.mjs         text eol=lf
*.js          text eol=lf
*.ts          text eol=lf
*.tsx         text eol=lf
*.json        text eol=lf
*.yml         text eol=lf
*.yaml        text eol=lf
*.conf        text eol=lf
*.service     text eol=lf
*.md          text eol=lf
Dockerfile    text eol=lf
.dockerignore text eol=lf
.gitignore    text eol=lf

# Never mangle these: a stray byte breaks the image outright.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.svg text eol=lf
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary

# Lockfiles are generated; diffing them is noise.
package-lock.json -diff linguist-generated
